<%#
  Shown to a visitor whose address places them in Bangladesh, once, offering
  them the site in their own language.

  WHY IT ASKS RATHER THAN SWITCHES. The owner's instruction was "check the IP
  by the backend and if users from Bangladesh then ask the user if they want to
  continue with English or switch to Bangla with a prompt" -- ask, not decide.
  A Bangladeshi developer who reads technical English all day and lands on a
  page that has silently become Bangla has had something taken from them; the
  same person offered the choice has been recognised. Geo is a guess and their
  click is a fact, which is the same rule the currency follows.

  IT APPEARS ONCE. Dismissing it, or choosing either language, writes
  `vh_lang_asked` and it never shows again -- a banner that returns on every
  page is an argument, not an offer. The choice itself goes through the normal
  switcher (/lang/bn), so it is remembered exactly like a click on the footer.

  It is rendered server-side only when it applies, so nobody else's page
  carries the markup, and it needs no JavaScript to be dismissed correctly --
  the form posts if scripting is off.
%>
<% if (typeof country !== 'undefined' && country === 'BD' && locale !== 'bn') { %>
  <div class="lang-offer" id="lang-offer" hidden data-once="vh_lang_asked">
    <div class="lang-offer-inner">
      <p class="lang-offer-text">
        <span class="lang-offer-bn" lang="bn">আপনি কি বাংলায় পড়তে চান?</span>
        <span class="lang-offer-en">Would you rather read this in Bangla?</span>
      </p>
      <div class="lang-offer-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-sm" href="<%= langHref('bn') %>" data-lang-offer="bn" lang="bn">বাংলায় দেখুন</a>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm" data-lang-offer="en">Keep English</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
<% } %>
